.TH TMUX-COMMAND-PALETTE 1 "2026-11-12" "tmux-tools" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tmux-command-palette \- pick a tmux key binding by its note and run it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tmux-command-palette
.B \-\-client
.I client
.br
.B tmux-command-palette
.B \-\-list
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tmux-command-palette
lists the key bindings of the
.B root
and
.B prefix
tables that carry a note
.RB ( "bind-key \-N" )
in an
.BR fzf (1)
popup on
.IR client .
Each entry shows the note first and the key second, such as
.BR "Copy the last N command outputs   prefix O" ,
sorted by note, so typing part of a description finds the action and shows
the key that runs it.
.PP
Enter runs the chosen binding; Escape closes the popup and runs nothing. The
popup only picks: once it has closed, the key is handed to the client as if it
had been typed, so the binding acts on the client's current pane, which is the
pane the palette was opened from. A prompt, confirmation, menu or popup that
the binding opens therefore appears over that pane, not inside the palette.
.PP
Bind it with the client that pressed the key:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bind -n M-Space run-shell 'tmux-command-palette --client #{q:client_name}'
.fi
.RE
.PP
The list leaves out entries that cannot or need not be replayed: mouse
bindings, which need a pointer position; tmux's own
.B Select window
.IR N
notes on
.B prefix 0
to
.BR "prefix 9" ;
and a second key with the same note as an earlier one, where the prefix key
is kept. Copy-mode and other key tables are not listed. The palette's own
binding stays in the list, as a command palette lists itself.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-client " client"
The tmux client to open the popup on and to replay the key for, usually
.B #{q:client_name}
from the binding.
.TP
.B \-\-list
Print the entries the popup would offer and exit. Each line holds the visible
text (note, padding and key), the key table and the key, separated by tabs.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
Replaying a key needs
.B send-keys \-K
from tmux 3.4. On older tmux the command cannot type for the client: after a
pick it turns the prefix on for a prefix binding and shows a message naming
the key to press, so one key press runs the binding. tmux 3.2 cannot target a
message at a client, so there the message goes to the session's most recently
active client.
.PP
Without
.BR fzf (1)
on
.BR PATH ,
the command shows a tmux message saying so and exits successfully.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
A binding was replayed or named, the popup was cancelled, or a missing
.BR fzf (1)
or an empty list was reported through tmux.
.TP
.B 1
tmux could not open the popup, for example for an unknown client, or another
tmux command failed.
.TP
.B 64
The arguments were invalid.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux (1),
.BR fzf (1)
